</div>

## <div style="color:#1a5276; font-size:20px; font-weight:bold; margin-top:20px; text-align:center;">LESSON IDENTIFICATION TABLE</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4 of 5</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 6th Grade (CEFR: A2.1)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> An Environmental Project</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Let's Recycle Our Paper!</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: productive: Draft descriptive texts about everyday events. interactive: Write short scripts for plays collaboratively about familiar topics.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can draft short scripts about everyday recycling activities (e.g., 'We wash the plastic and glass bottles every day.'). Can write descriptive texts on recycling projects (e.g., 'We collect the plastic bottles and sort them by color.'). Can collaborate with peers to write and perform short scripts about environmental projects (e.g., 'Let's write a play about how we recycle at school. I'll write the first part, and you can add the next part.').</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

## <div style="color:#1a5276; font-size:20px; font-weight:bold; margin-top:20px; text-align:center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 40%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 45%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 15%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color:#1a5276;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher shows pictures of different types of waste (paper, plastic, glass) and recycling bins. Students write down two things they can recycle from the pictures. The teacher asks, "What do we do with old paper?" and encourages short written responses on mini whiteboards or scrap paper.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher writes a simple sentence on the board: "We collect paper for our project." Then, demonstrates how to write a simple instruction: "Put paper in the blue bin." The teacher models the pronunciation of 'will' /wɪl/ and 'I'll' /aɪl/ by saying, "I'll write about our project now. We will write scripts today." and points to the words as she says them.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Are we writing about a past event or a plan for the future?" (Expected answer: A plan for the future, or everyday events).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When I say 'Put paper in the blue bin,' is that an instruction or a question?" (Expected answer: An instruction).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color:#1a5276;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short dialogue script (3-4 lines) between two students planning a recycling project, incorporating present simple, future simple, and imperatives. Also, a short descriptive text (3-4 sentences) about everyday recycling activities in a classroom. Genre: Short scripts and descriptive texts; Register: Informal/instructional; Format: Written text on a slide or handout.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><i>Example Script:</i></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Ana:</b> "Hi Carlos! We have a big recycling <b>project</b> this week."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Carlos:</b> "Yes! We <b>will clean</b> the <b>classroom</b> first. We <b>collect</b> paper every day."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Ana:</b> "Great! Remember, <b>put</b> the paper in the blue bin."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><i>Example Descriptive Text:</i></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">"Our school has an environmental <b>project</b>. We <b>recycle</b> paper every day. We <b>will plant trees</b> in the <b>garden</b> next month. This <b>plan</b> is important for our <b>environment</b>."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Interpret short, descriptive texts and simple instructions related to environmental projects.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students read the example script/text and underline all verbs in the present simple (e.g., collect, recycle).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students identify two instructions from the script/text (e.g., "Put the paper in the blue bin.").</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">8-10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color:#1a5276;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Writing)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice writing sentences using the future simple tense for plans, imperatives for instructions, and present simple to describe everyday events. Target vocabulary: project, environment, classroom, plan, garden, trees, plants, meeting, event, participate, clean, plant, grow, water, dig, care, prepared, excited, ready, future, upcoming.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice: Sentence Completion.</b> The teacher provides sentences with blanks. Students complete them using the correct form of the verb in parentheses (present simple, future simple) or an appropriate imperative, and target vocabulary. E.g., "We ___ (collect) plastic bottles every week." "Tomorrow, we ___ (plant) new ____ (trees)." "___ (Bring) your old newspapers to the ____ (classroom)."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Practice: Picture Prompts & Sentence Building.</b> Students are shown 3-4 simple pictures related to recycling or planting. For each picture, they write two sentences: one describing an everyday action using the present simple, and one giving an instruction using an imperative. E.g., (Picture of sorting paper) "We sort paper every day. Put the paper here." (Picture of planting a tree) "We water the plants. Dig a hole." The teacher reminds students to segment multisyllabic words like 'presentation' into 'pre-sen-ta-tion' if they struggle with spelling longer words.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction: Collaborative Instructions.</b> In pairs, students imagine they are creating a poster with instructions for their "Let's Recycle Our Paper!" project. They take turns writing one instruction or one descriptive sentence about the project, collaboratively building a short paragraph (3-4 sentences) that includes at least one imperative, one present simple sentence, and one future simple sentence. Example: "First, <b>collect</b> the paper. We <b>put</b> it in the big blue bin. Next week, we <b>will make</b> recycled paper."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15-18 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color:#1a5276;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative Script Drafting. Students will work in pairs or small groups (3 students) to draft a short script (5-7 lines) for a play about recycling paper in their school or community. The script must include: suggestions, instructions, descriptions of everyday events, and future plans related to recycling paper or an environmental project. Students are encouraged to use the target grammar (present simple, future simple, imperatives) and vocabulary (project, environment, classroom, plan, trees, plants, participate, clean, care, excited, ready, future).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students brainstorm ideas for their short play: Who are the characters? What is the problem (e.g., too much paper waste)? What is the solution? (e.g., recycling, planting new trees). They will discuss environmental actions and propose ways to reduce paper waste, integrating the sociolinguistic competence.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each group drafts their script. Students are reminded to explain how to recycle and give instructions and suggestions (pragmatic competence). E.g., "Let's recycle paper! We usually sort the bottles by color and size. Tomorrow, we will start our new paper collection!"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher circulates, offering support, checking for use of target language, and providing feedback on clarity and coherence of the written script.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10-12 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color:#1a5276;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist and peer assessment.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students exchange their drafted scripts with another group. Each group reviews the other's script based on a simple checklist provided by the teacher.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Peer Checklist):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Did the script include at least one instruction (imperative)? (e.g., 'Bring paper.')</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Did the script describe an everyday recycling activity (present simple)? (e.g., 'We clean bottles.')</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Did the script include a future plan? (e.g., 'We will plant trees.')</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Was the script clear and easy to understand?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Did they use at least three vocabulary words from our list (e.g., project, environment, plan)?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a quick exit ticket before leaving:</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One thing I learned about writing instructions/plans today is..."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can now write a sentence about recycling using the word 'will'." (e.g., 'I will recycle paper.')</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color:#1a5276;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of writing the script was most challenging for you?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How can we use these types of sentences (instructions, plans, descriptions) in other environmental projects?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> Most students successfully drafted short scripts, incorporating imperatives and present simple tense. Some struggled with consistent use of the future simple for plans, often mixing it with present simple. The collaborative activity in pairs worked well to encourage peer support. I need to provide more explicit examples and a structured sentence starter activity for future simple sentences in the next lesson's writing task. I will also review the pronunciation of 'will' and 'I'll' by having students read their scripts aloud briefly in the next lesson.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> The scripts drafted today will be used as a basis for the "Mediation & Integration" lesson (Lesson 5), where students will refine their scripts, potentially perform them, and integrate the information into a larger project presentation, focusing on summarizing and explaining their environmental actions.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">2-3 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>